byid['1975-375.7']['statement']=r'For the notebook\'s matrix equation \(\Lambda^{\prime}=[\Lambda,U-EA]\), direct differentiation proves only that the coefficients of \(\det(zI-\Lambda(E))\) are constants of motion. A claim of Liouville integrability additionally requires a specified Poisson manifold or symplectic leaf, commuting integrals and a rank count. The notebook supplies neither the Poisson bracket nor that phase space; consequently its full Hamiltonian assertion is only partially identifiable here. No complete modern integrability theorem for unspecified stationary data is substituted for it.'
byid['1975-375.7']['reconstruction']=r'The exact consequence of the written Lax equation is separated from the stronger publication theorem. The latter has not been reproduced with all of its phase-space and independence hypotheses, so publication matching remains partial.'
